Version: 1.0.0-draft

1. Purpose

The Atlas Core Concepts (ACC) establish the canonical conceptual foundation of the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework.

The purpose of this specification is to define the fundamental engineering concepts, their meanings, relationships, and governing principles that are shared across all Atlas specifications and implementations.

These concepts provide a common vocabulary and conceptual model that ensures consistency, interoperability, and architectural integrity throughout the Atlas ecosystem.

2. Scope

This specification defines the canonical concepts that describe engineering cognition independently of any software implementation, programming language, database, artificial intelligence model, or engineering discipline.

The Atlas Core Concepts apply uniformly to all Atlas specifications, including but not limited to:

Atlas Hivemind Specification (AHS)
Engineering Understanding Specification (EUS)
Engineering Memory Specification (EMS)
Engineering Knowledge Specification (EKS)
Engineering Evidence Specification (EES)
Engineering Reasoning Specification (ERS)
Engineering Capability Specification (ECS)
Decision Support Specification (DSS)
3. Objectives

The Atlas Core Concepts SHALL:

Establish a shared engineering vocabulary.
Define the canonical meaning of each core concept.
Describe the relationships between concepts.
Eliminate conflicting definitions across specifications.
Provide a stable conceptual foundation for future evolution.
4. Principles

The Atlas Core Concepts are governed by the following principles:

Canonical

Each concept SHALL have one authoritative definition.

Consistent

A concept SHALL maintain the same meaning throughout all Atlas specifications.

Technology Independent

Concepts SHALL not depend upon any implementation technology.

Domain Independent

Concepts SHALL remain applicable across engineering disciplines.

Extensible

New concepts MAY be introduced without altering the meaning of existing concepts.

Traceable

Relationships between concepts SHALL be explicitly defined.

5. Relationships

The Atlas Core Concepts serve as the conceptual foundation for every Atlas specification.

All normative specifications SHALL reference the definitions established by the Atlas Core Concepts rather than redefining core terminology.

6. Normative Statements

The Atlas Core Concepts SHALL be regarded as the authoritative source for all fundamental terminology used throughout the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework.

No Atlas specification SHALL redefine a concept established by this specification in a manner that changes its canonical meaning.

7. Non-Goals

The Atlas Core Concepts do not:

Define software architecture.
Specify implementation details.
Prescribe algorithms or technologies.
Replace domain-specific engineering standards.
Define operational workflows.